  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Unit Step as the Integral of an Impulse",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert the accumulated integral of a shifted unit impulse into a shifted unit step by identifying when the impulse area has been included.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Unit Step as the Integral of an Impulse",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Unit Step as the Integral of an Impulse",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Let $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}\\delta(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$. This is an accumulation problem: it counts how much of the unit impulse's area has been included as the upper limit moves past $\\tau=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}\\delta(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Before the limit reaches the impulse, nothing has been accumulated, so $y(t)=0$ for $t<0$. Once the limit reaches or passes the impulse, the full unit area is included, so $y(t)=1$ for $t\\ge 0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That $0$-to-$1$ switch is exactly the unit step $u(t)$, so the accumulated impulse is written as $y(t)=u(t)$. Read this as the basic switch rule: off before $0$, on at and after $0$, with the matching reverse relation $\\frac{d}{dt}u(t)=\\delta(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this as the base case for shifted impulses later: the only thing that changes is the time at which the accumulation window first includes the impulse area.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Unit Step as the Integral of an Impulse",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Accumulating an Unshifted Unit Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_{-\\infty}^{t} \\delta(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ and write the result as a unit-step expression.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the unit impulse at the origin. Let $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}\\delta(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$, so the task is to decide when the moving upper limit has included the unit area of $\\delta(\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}\\delta(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $t<0$, the interval $(-\\infty,t]$ stops before $\\tau=0$, so no impulse area has been included and $y(t)=0$. For $t\\ge 0$, the interval includes the impulse, so the full unit area has been included and $y(t)=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0, & t<0 \\\\ 1, & t\\ge 0\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the accumulated impulse is exactly the unit step: $y(t)=u(t)$. This is the baseline unshifted case that later examples will shift to other impulse locations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Unit Step as the Integral of an Impulse",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Accumulating a Shifted Unit Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}\\delta(\\tau-3)\\,d\\tau$ and express the result as a unit step.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted unit impulse at $\\tau=3$. Let $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}\\delta(\\tau-3)\\,d\\tau$, so the task is to decide when the moving upper limit has included the impulse area at that shift point.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}\\delta(\\tau-3)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $t<3$, the interval $(-\\infty,t]$ stops before $\\tau=3$, so no impulse area has been included and $y(t)=0$. For $t\\ge 3$, the interval includes the impulse, so the full unit area has been included and $y(t)=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0, & t<3 \\\\ 1, & t\\ge 3\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the accumulated shifted impulse is exactly the shifted unit step: $y(t)=u(t-3)$. The only thing that changed from the unshifted case is the time at which the accumulation window first includes the unit area.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Unit Step as the Integral of an Impulse",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Deciding Whether the Impulse Has Been Included",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Decide if the impulse at $\\tau=2$ has been accumulated in $\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}\\delta(\\tau-2)\\,d\\tau$ for $t=-1,2,5$ and state the matching step form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted unit impulse centered at $\\tau=2$. Let $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}\\delta(\\tau-2)\\,d\\tau$, so the task is to test whether the moving upper limit has reached the shift point before labeling the output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}\\delta(\\tau-2)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $t=-1$, the interval $(-\\infty,t]$ stops before $\\tau=2$, so the impulse has not been included and the value is $0$. For $t=2$, the interval reaches the impulse location, so the unit area is included and the value is $1$. For $t=5$, the interval is already past the impulse, so the value is still $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0, & t<2 \\\\ 1, & t\\ge 2\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the inclusion test switches exactly at $t=2$, which means the accumulated impulse is the shifted unit step $u(t-2)$. The rule to carry forward is to compare the moving upper limit to the impulse location, then write $0$ before inclusion and $1$ at or after inclusion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Unit Step as the Integral of an Impulse",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Converting Scaled Impulse Accumulations into Scaled Steps",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $\\int_{-\\infty}^{t} 5\\delta(\\tau+1)\\,d\\tau$ into its unit-step form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a scaled impulse centered at $\\tau=-1$. Use linearity first: the factor $5$ stays attached while the accumulation test still checks whether the moving upper limit has reached the impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{t} 5\\delta(\\tau+1)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $t<-1$, the interval $(-\\infty,t]$ stops before $\\tau=-1$, so the impulse has not been included and the value is $0$. For $t\\ge -1$, the interval includes the full unit area, so the scale factor carries through and the value is $5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0, & t<-1 \\\\ 5, & t\\ge -1\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the accumulated scaled impulse is $y(t)=5u(t+1)$. The rule to carry forward is simple: keep the coefficient, check the shifted include point, and then write the corresponding scaled step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
